  version             
  help                
  name-rev            
  refs                
  merge-file          
  mktree              
  mktag               
  unpack-file         
  get-tar-commit-id   
  update-index        
  checkout-index      
  read-tree           
  diff-tree           
  diff-index          
  diff-files          
  show-index          
  verify-pack         
  index-pack          
  unpack-objects      
  prune-packed        
  pack-objects        
  repack              
  commit-graph        
  multi-pack-index    
  fast-export         
  fast-import         
  sparse-checkout     
  submodule           
  undo                Undo the last rgit operation (commit, merge, reset --hard, rebase, ...); `--list` shows the log
  redo                Bring back the operation most recently undone
  oops                "I just did something wrong": lists the most likely fixes; `rgit oops <n>` runs one
  wip                 Save everything uncommitted as a WIP commit on a hidden per-branch ref and clean the tree; `--restore` brings it back [alias: save]
  fixup               Fold the staged changes into an earlier commit (picked from a list or named) and rewrite the commits on top
  sync                Fetch the branch's upstream, print the plan, then fast-forward, rebase or merge onto it (and push with --push)
  absorb              Fold each staged hunk into the commit that last changed those lines (blame), in one rewrite
  split               Split one commit into several by hunk, picking each new commit's hunks and message
  whereami            One screen on where you are: branch, upstream, last commit, changes, operation in progress, saves, and what to run next
  move-commit         Move commits of the current branch to another branch (cherry-pick there, drop here) without switching
  ignore              Add patterns to .gitignore (or the global ignore file) and stop tracking the files they now ignore
  story               The history of a file or directory in plain words: created, renames, rewrites, authors, who wrote it today, timeline
  hotspots            Files that change most, have the most authors, and change together (coupling)
  doctor              Check the repository for problems (objects, refs, identity, config, hooks, stale locks, worktrees, huge files) and how to fix them
  stats               Numbers on the branch's history: activity, weekdays, authors, languages, biggest commits
  timeline            A day-by-day digest of the repository: commits, authors, branches, merges, lines
  compare             Where two branches stand: ahead, behind or diverged, the commits and files on each side, and whether merging would conflict
  secrets             Look for secrets (keys, tokens, passwords, .env files) in the staged changes, all files or the history; exit 1 when found

Options:
      --explain  Narrate what the command actually did, and what it means (`rgit --explain merge x`)
      --teach    Like --explain, plus the refs and index changes underneath
      --plain    Exactly git's output, no rgit narration
  -h, --help     Print help
  -V, --version  Print version
